1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,284
They're a passport to adventure.

2
00:00:02,285 --> 00:00:08,280
These vehicles are equally at home, on the road, or trekking into the great unknown.

3
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:14,460
Whether it's for adrenaline-pumping experiences or finding that remote, unspoiled getaway, these autos deliver.

4
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:18,360
Now, 4x4s on Modern Marbles, then and now.

5
00:00:18,361 --> 00:00:28,560
There's something liberating in the ability to leave the posted highways

6
00:00:28,561 --> 00:00:30,920
and forge your own way through the world.

7
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:36,380
In recent years, four-wheel drive sport utility vehicles

8
00:00:36,381 --> 00:00:40,120
have made these cross-country treks possible for almost anyone.

9
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:47,040
Yet at the beginning of the 20th century,

10
00:00:47,041 --> 00:00:52,000
almost every excursion in an automobile was automatically an off-road adventure.

11
00:00:56,060 --> 00:00:58,060
Only city streets were paved,

12
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:02,740
and these were still dominated by pedestrians, horses, and trolley cars.

13
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:09,100
Once outside the city, automobiles were on their own.

14
00:01:09,980 --> 00:01:12,980
This meant that early cars had to be able to go anywhere.

15
00:01:12,981 --> 00:01:19,500
and go anywhere they did, or at least they tried.

16
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:24,660
From picnic afternoons to cross-country treks for more adventurous drivers,

17
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:29,200
daily excursions often meant a trip to the bottom of the nearest mud hole.

18
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:34,640
I guess we don't realize what it was like because of the way we look at everything today

19
00:01:34,641 --> 00:01:38,960
and everything's so accessible, but back then there were very few public roads

20
00:01:38,961 --> 00:01:46,640
and they were no more than horse-drawn carriage trails in the area and all over the country, really.

21
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:53,540
I think you really, in the early days, had to be an absolutely adventurous spirit,

22
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:57,440
possibly even more so than the people that four-wheel today.

23
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:06,800
Until the mid to late 1920s, there weren't particularly good or extensive road system in the country,

24
00:02:06,801 --> 00:02:09,260
not a lot of services

25
00:02:09,261 --> 00:02:12,900
you basically had to know how to handle your vehicle

26
00:02:12,901 --> 00:02:14,180
how to repair your vehicle

27
00:02:14,181 --> 00:02:16,120
it was an adventure

28
00:02:16,121 --> 00:02:19,640
although it did its best

29
00:02:19,641 --> 00:02:21,260
the car had its limitations

30
00:02:21,261 --> 00:02:23,860
with only two wheels providing power

31
00:02:23,861 --> 00:02:28,440
slippery roads, mud, snow and hills were a problem

32
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:45,540
At the turn of the century, when cars were invented, there weren't really roads.

33
00:02:45,980 --> 00:02:48,560
There were two ruts that wagons had been pulled over,

34
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:52,060
and when you tried to drive a mechanical vehicle over there with very thin wheels,

35
00:02:52,420 --> 00:02:56,900
you didn't have any traction, and a two-wheel drive was very limited in what you could actually do with it.

36
00:02:57,320 --> 00:02:59,260
Hence the need for a four-wheel drive system.

37
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:04,980
As early as 1899, automotive pioneer Robert E. Twyford of Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania,

38
00:03:04,981 --> 00:03:09,080
was employing a primitive four-wheel drive system on a variety of cars.

39
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:13,860
Others followed with their own designs, but the idea remained little more than a novelty.

40
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:15,760
Yeah, there was a lot of experimentation.

41
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:19,880
I've heard people say there were up to 35 different people and companies

42
00:03:19,881 --> 00:03:24,300
working on developing automobiles and four-wheel drive systems,

43
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:26,320
something to replace the horse and the carriage.

44
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:33,400
It wasn't until 1906 that the first commercially viable four-wheel drive vehicle

45
00:03:33,401 --> 00:03:35,680
hit the muddy roads of North America.

46
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:40,040
That year, the Duplex Power Car Company of Charlotte, Michigan,

47
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:41,360
developed the Model B,

48
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:45,380
a 14-horsepower, two-cylinder, four-wheel drive truck.

49
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:53,000
But the real breakthrough in four-wheel drive motoring came in 1908,

50
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:57,760
when two Wisconsin machinists, Otto Zakow and William Besserdick,

51
00:03:58,120 --> 00:04:00,840
built their first steam-powered 4x4.

52
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:14,660
They were pretty much the people everybody brought things to in town to fix, repair, or kind of change.

53
00:04:14,661 --> 00:04:20,280
And it seems like they were always interested in advancement or trying to improve things.

54
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:28,260
And that was basically what the four-wheel drive concept stemmed from, is their inventive nature.

55
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:32,640
And just trying to figure out how to make something work better.

56
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:44,820
The car broke new ground with the first steerable, integrated front-driving axle in America.

57
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:55,060
The following year, Zakow and Besserdick replaced the steam engine with a gas-powered 45-horsepower four-cylinder power plant.

58
00:04:55,200 --> 00:05:00,120
They attached a red body to the chassis and dubbed the car the Battleship.

59
00:05:00,121 --> 00:05:04,340
They vigorously tested the vehicle.

60
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:06,640
The design proved to be battle-ready,

61
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:09,800
and they formed the Badger 4-Wheel Drive Company

62
00:05:09,801 --> 00:05:12,960
and tried to sell their all-weather, all-road car.

63
00:05:14,120 --> 00:05:15,520
Results were mixed.

64
00:05:16,120 --> 00:05:19,560
Initially, 4-Wheel Drive just about doubled the cost of the vehicle.

65
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:24,980
So you had the choice of spending almost twice as much for getting stuck.

66
00:05:24,981 --> 00:05:30,080
And for some people, it was a lot cheaper to get stuck than it was to buy 4-Wheel Drive.

67
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:33,080
And that did slow the development of four-wheel drive more than anything.

68
00:05:35,500 --> 00:05:39,740
In 1910, Walter A. Olin took over the struggling company

69
00:05:39,741 --> 00:05:43,140
and changed the name to the Four-Wheel Drive Company,

70
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:45,580
later shortened to FWD.

71
00:05:46,740 --> 00:05:50,160
Until W.A. Olin came along, it was just another idea

72
00:05:50,161 --> 00:05:54,500
or an invention in a blacksmith shop that somebody was playing with.

73
00:05:54,501 --> 00:06:04,880
Olin was so sure of his car that he offered a $1,000 prize to any vehicle that could follow the battleship cross-country for 15 minutes.

74
00:06:08,900 --> 00:06:13,780
Hundreds of cars accepted the challenge, but no one ever collected the prize.

75
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:19,960
Although the car had proved itself structurally, the battleship still didn't find a market with motorists.

76
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:23,000
And only seven were produced.

77
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:26,440
There wasn't a lot of wealth in this area.

78
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:33,700
There were a lot of farmers and loggers, not a lot of wealthy people.

79
00:06:34,280 --> 00:06:39,160
And it was at that time considered to be not a necessity, but a luxury.

80
00:06:39,860 --> 00:06:42,800
And a relatively expensive luxury for that time.

81
00:06:44,220 --> 00:06:47,740
They were very good cars, but people just didn't have the money to spend.

82
00:06:47,741 --> 00:06:52,120
And in the end, they discovered that the truck market was booming.

83
00:06:52,121 --> 00:06:56,920
and the all-wheel drive truck market was virtually untapped.

84
00:06:57,320 --> 00:07:00,840
At the vanguard of the burgeoning truck market was the U.S. Army,

85
00:07:00,841 --> 00:07:04,220
which began to experiment with trucks for military operations.

86
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:08,920
In 1911, a cross-country test was scheduled

87
00:07:08,921 --> 00:07:13,100
to see if, indeed, motorized vehicles could replace horses.

88
00:07:13,101 --> 00:07:27,101
FWD's entry, a stripped-down battleship, was the only vehicle employing four-wheel drive,

89
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:31,480
which gave it a leg up on the other three trucks in the 1,500-mile test.

90
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:35,400
It was even called upon to pull the competition out of the mud.

91
00:07:35,401 --> 00:07:44,480
So by the time that test was over, everybody was pretty convinced that a four-wheel drive was in the best interest of the Army

92
00:07:44,481 --> 00:07:47,880
because it could handle terrain that nothing else could.

93
00:07:49,340 --> 00:07:53,760
Even though four-wheel drive had proven it could meet the demanding needs of the military,

94
00:07:54,140 --> 00:07:58,840
it took several years for the government to call upon the tough trucks for active duty.

95
00:07:58,841 --> 00:08:06,800
Initially, the Army didn't get too involved with FWD.

96
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:13,000
It wasn't until about 1916 that they started buying FWD Model B trucks in large numbers.

97
00:08:13,001 --> 00:08:21,400
And that was mostly for, in 1916, when Pancho Villa started raiding across the border in the southwest,

98
00:08:22,220 --> 00:08:28,560
they sent General John Blackjack Pershing to chase down Pancho Villa.

99
00:08:28,840 --> 00:08:32,080
and they decided that they were going to make this a motorized campaign.

100
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:38,860
And it was the first actual test of motor trucks and four-wheel drive trucks in an actual military campaign.

101
00:08:41,580 --> 00:08:44,140
By the time World War I broke out in Europe,

102
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:51,140
four-wheel and two-wheel drive trucks were widely accepted as troop transport vehicles and ambulances.

103
00:08:52,140 --> 00:08:56,880
It was clear that a mechanized army could travel much farther and much faster

104
00:08:56,881 --> 00:09:00,320
and over much more treacherous terrain than ever before.

105
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:06,000
By 1940, with the introduction of the Dodge VC,

106
00:09:06,820 --> 00:09:09,600
the government was ready to completely transform the Army

107
00:09:09,601 --> 00:09:11,800
into a modern, mobile fighting machine

108
00:09:11,801 --> 00:09:15,200
and say goodbye to the horse forever.

109
00:09:15,960 --> 00:09:18,280
The Army, between World War I and World War II,

110
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:21,820
recognized that the horse was becoming an outdated mode of transportation.

111
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:25,540
A four-wheeled vehicle, a four-wheeled drive vehicle,

112
00:09:25,541 --> 00:09:28,720
could adapt to different varieties, different situations.

113
00:09:29,080 --> 00:09:31,220
It could hold more people. It could carry more ammo.

114
00:09:31,340 --> 00:09:33,660
It didn't get tired. It didn't get sick, so to speak.

115
00:09:34,660 --> 00:09:40,140
The VC series was offered in a command car, a pickup, and a carry-all body style.

116
00:09:41,100 --> 00:09:43,560
These rugged transport vehicles could go anywhere,

117
00:09:43,960 --> 00:09:47,280
but were not small or agile enough to be used in combat.

118
00:09:55,540 --> 00:10:02,540
When Hitler's blitzkrieg of Poland began in 1939, the face of warfare changed forever.

119
00:10:02,541 --> 00:10:10,540
It was easy to see how valuable a more nimble go-anywhere vehicle could be in the struggle to defeat the Germans.

120
00:10:10,541 --> 00:10:16,540
All of Europe was scrambling to stay ahead of the advancing German army.

121
00:10:17,300 --> 00:10:26,020
Meanwhile, in the United States, a project was underway to develop a tough, fast, all-terrain vehicle that could outrun and outshoot the Nazis.

122
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:32,460
The result would forever change the course of modern warfare and change the course of history.

123
00:10:34,580 --> 00:10:42,180
The Kubelwagen, developed from the 1930s Volkswagen, was one of the most effective vehicles in Germany's arsenal during World War II.

124
00:10:42,820 --> 00:10:44,540
4x4 will continue in a moment.

125
00:10:44,541 --> 00:10:49,780
You're watching 4x4 on Modern Marbles, then and now.

126
00:10:53,360 --> 00:11:00,680
After World War I, America's drive to replace the horse with a new military transportation system was moving slowly.

127
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:05,640
Large trucks were fine for transportation of troops.

128
00:11:06,380 --> 00:11:13,700
But by the 1930s, some were lobbying for a light-duty combat vehicle that could get soldiers in and out of tight spots.

129
00:11:15,420 --> 00:11:19,240
It was a tough sell for a country still mired in a depression.

130
00:11:21,140 --> 00:11:24,180
You have to remember in those days that the budgets were minuscule.

131
00:11:24,440 --> 00:11:28,580
The politicians were not letting any money loose at all.

132
00:11:28,700 --> 00:11:35,680
So whatever development the Army did, they had to do it by circumventing normal modes of funding

133
00:11:35,681 --> 00:11:40,140
and finding the money elsewhere and doing it themselves.

134
00:11:40,141 --> 00:11:45,800
Captain Robert C. Howe and Sergeant M. C. Wiley took up the challenge.

135
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:49,860
Yet their belly flopper proved to be an impractical solution.

136
00:11:50,660 --> 00:11:52,700
The Army, however, had other ideas.

137
00:11:57,580 --> 00:12:02,380
And they wanted a really light scout car that would carry two or three men.

138
00:12:02,520 --> 00:12:03,220
That's all they wanted.

139
00:12:03,320 --> 00:12:04,540
Maybe a light machine gun.

140
00:12:04,980 --> 00:12:06,080
Be relatively fast.

141
00:12:06,280 --> 00:12:08,480
They wanted it to be able to replace the motorcycle.

142
00:12:08,940 --> 00:12:11,380
Now, the motorcycle had been in use since World War I,

143
00:12:11,980 --> 00:12:16,080
and they found that it was very difficult to use a motorcycle cross-country.

144
00:12:16,081 --> 00:12:22,200
You had to be a very good rider to exact the kind of performance that was needed out of a motorcycle and not get hurt.

145
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:24,100
They had a lot of injuries with motorcycles.

146
00:12:24,980 --> 00:12:33,080
So they developed a set of specifications for a scout car.

147
00:12:33,420 --> 00:12:37,220
Now, initially, those specifications did not necessarily include four-wheel drive,

148
00:12:37,221 --> 00:12:48,100
But they went to a company that specialized in building small vehicles, a company called the American Bantam Car Company in Butler, Pennsylvania.

149
00:12:51,060 --> 00:13:00,160
Bantam had been building the American version of the Austin 7, a small British roadster that was popular everywhere, except in the United States.

150
00:13:01,080 --> 00:13:03,500
Unable to sell the car to American buyers,

151
00:13:04,080 --> 00:13:06,460
the company pinned its hopes on proving to the government

152
00:13:06,461 --> 00:13:09,240
it could build a new, lightweight combat vehicle.

153
00:13:11,680 --> 00:13:17,020
In the late 30s, Bantam had built a series of a few cars

154
00:13:17,021 --> 00:13:21,180
for the Pennsylvania National Guard using their two-wheel drive chassis.

155
00:13:22,100 --> 00:13:27,800
And as the military interest heightened on a vehicle for quarter-ton four-wheel drive,

156
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:32,460
Bantam invited the Army to come and see what they had built on the two-wheel drive chassis.

157
00:13:34,380 --> 00:13:38,200
They were anxious to make a government contract of some sort.

158
00:13:38,760 --> 00:13:45,480
So the Army sent a team of experts to Bantam to look at the factory, look at the vehicles,

159
00:13:45,481 --> 00:13:51,360
and see if Bantam could build something that fit their specifications.

160
00:13:52,140 --> 00:13:55,800
So they had sort of a two- or three-day engineering jam session

161
00:13:55,801 --> 00:13:57,920
where they discussed a whole lot of ideas.

162
00:13:58,780 --> 00:14:00,860
Bantam, along with the Army engineers,

163
00:14:01,200 --> 00:14:04,860
drew up the basic specifications for a quarter-ton reconnaissance vehicle.

164
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:09,080
Official plans were then drafted, and a bid contract was sent out.

165
00:14:10,100 --> 00:14:12,260
Bantam felt they had a leg up on the competition.

166
00:14:13,280 --> 00:14:16,260
But the bid went out to 135 companies,

167
00:14:16,540 --> 00:14:18,240
including Willys Overland,

168
00:14:18,560 --> 00:14:21,160
who had made a name for themselves in the 1920s

169
00:14:21,161 --> 00:14:24,020
with the Whippet and its powerful Go Devil engine.

170
00:14:25,800 --> 00:14:30,880
Unfortunately, Bantam's bid was not the lowest bid.

171
00:14:31,780 --> 00:14:35,340
Willys' Overland bid was a little bit lower than Bantam's,

172
00:14:35,740 --> 00:14:41,560
but Willys was not willing to meet the 49-day schedule for a pilot model vehicle,

173
00:14:41,561 --> 00:14:45,760
and that's what threw the contract to Bantam.

174
00:14:46,780 --> 00:14:50,100
Bantam won the initial bid to produce a working prototype.

175
00:14:50,620 --> 00:14:54,140
If approved, they would build 70 more vehicles for further tests.

176
00:14:54,960 --> 00:14:57,640
While the Army was pleased with the Bantam design,

177
00:14:58,100 --> 00:15:03,740
the top brass had doubts about the company's ability to produce enough of the vehicles to meet the Army's demands.

178
00:15:05,180 --> 00:15:09,960
They did a pretty good job of building the vehicles that they were contracted to build,

179
00:15:10,640 --> 00:15:15,680
but to produce thousands and thousands and thousands of vehicles in a short period of time,

180
00:15:16,040 --> 00:15:18,240
they weren't sure that Bantam could do that.

181
00:15:18,241 --> 00:15:20,840
There was a big machine tool shortage at that time,

182
00:15:20,841 --> 00:15:27,420
and they gave preference to companies that had the ability to produce certain parts in-house

183
00:15:27,421 --> 00:15:35,940
and Willys and Ford, who later got involved in the Jeep deal as well, were better able to do that.

184
00:15:35,941 --> 00:15:41,500
So it was purely a matter of practicality that Bantam was aced out of the Jeep business.

185
00:15:42,560 --> 00:15:47,220
Eventually, it was Willys and their go-devil engine that would grab the brass ring.

186
00:15:48,020 --> 00:15:51,720
But when the government began to manufacture the new vehicles in large numbers,

187
00:15:52,380 --> 00:15:56,180
Willys had to share the glory with longtime Army supplier Ford,

188
00:15:56,560 --> 00:15:59,060
which was better equipped to meet the government's demands.

189
00:16:02,360 --> 00:16:07,300
While everyone was in agreement that the new Willys MB was a revolutionary new vehicle,

190
00:16:07,880 --> 00:16:09,740
no one could agree on what to call it.

191
00:16:10,380 --> 00:16:16,500
Names like the Beep, the Peep, the Bug, the Chigger, the Gnat, the Midget,

192
00:16:16,501 --> 00:16:18,720
and the blitz buggy were suggested.

193
00:16:19,760 --> 00:16:22,660
Finally, the name Jeep won out.

194
00:16:24,740 --> 00:16:26,880
The term started in World War I,

195
00:16:26,940 --> 00:16:29,760
and it was a term that was used for either a new human recruit

196
00:16:29,761 --> 00:16:33,120
or a new military vehicle that came into the motor pool

197
00:16:33,121 --> 00:16:34,600
that people weren't familiar with.

198
00:16:35,720 --> 00:16:39,000
Some believe the name came from a popular comic character,

199
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:40,280
Eugene the Jeep,

200
00:16:40,760 --> 00:16:44,080
a magical creature who could go anywhere and do anything.

201
00:16:46,500 --> 00:16:52,920
So if you connect the dots, the term Jeep came about for both reasons.

202
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:59,480
On December 7, 1941, one month after the Willys MB went into production,

203
00:17:00,100 --> 00:17:03,720
America declared war after Japanese bombers attacked Pearl Harbor.

204
00:17:05,680 --> 00:17:08,460
America quickly mobilized against the Axis.

205
00:17:09,340 --> 00:17:11,820
But when it was first introduced to soldiers,

206
00:17:12,280 --> 00:17:16,240
no one knew quite what to make of the short, squat, green convertible.

207
00:17:16,500 --> 00:17:23,360
It wouldn't take long, however, for the GI to embrace the jeep as a tough, life-saving companion on the front lines.

208
00:17:24,520 --> 00:17:27,280
One of the jeep's first tests came in Burma.

209
00:17:27,740 --> 00:17:33,940
General Stilwell's troops were trapped between the advancing Japanese army and the rain-soaked mountains leading into India.

210
00:17:34,780 --> 00:17:40,580
The jeep performed above and beyond the call of duty, ferrying the troops safely to their destination.

211
00:17:41,440 --> 00:17:45,900
Across Europe and the Pacific, there seemed to be no task that the jeep wasn't up to.

212
00:17:46,500 --> 00:17:50,000
The jeep basically did everything during World War II.

213
00:17:51,020 --> 00:17:53,920
Moved troops, moved supplies.

214
00:17:54,360 --> 00:17:57,800
Some of them were fitted with railroad wheels to pull train cars.

215
00:17:58,460 --> 00:18:02,460
Just about anything you can imagine a wheeled vehicle could do, it did.

216
00:18:05,860 --> 00:18:09,440
Dignitaries visiting the troops were proud to be seen in the tough little trooper.

217
00:18:09,441 --> 00:18:14,040
King George, Queen Elizabeth and Franklin Roosevelt

218
00:18:14,041 --> 00:18:17,340
chose Jeeps over more luxurious modes of transportation

219
00:18:17,341 --> 00:18:18,980
on their trips into the field

220
00:18:18,981 --> 00:18:23,740
The cachet of the Jeep could be seen back in the States

221
00:18:23,741 --> 00:18:26,780
when Hollywood's elite boarded a wagon train of Jeeps

222
00:18:26,781 --> 00:18:28,500
to raise money for the war effort

223
00:18:28,501 --> 00:18:35,700
While overseas, the Jeep quickly became a soldier's best friend

224
00:18:35,701 --> 00:18:43,420
During the war, they really came to rely on these Jeeps to get them out of the situations that they dreaded,

225
00:18:43,460 --> 00:18:46,740
to take them through the worst situations you could possibly imagine.

226
00:18:47,300 --> 00:18:53,280
This vehicle became their friend, much akin to the relationship that they may have had to their horse prior.

227
00:18:53,900 --> 00:18:58,880
This vehicle became their best friend out in the field, a very dependable, life-saving instrument.

228
00:18:58,881 --> 00:19:05,860
By the end of the war in 1945, the Jeep was called America's greatest contribution to modern warfare.

229
00:19:07,160 --> 00:19:13,280
The stage was set for Willys to capitalize on the Allied victory and the Jeep's fame in the civilian marketplace.

230
00:19:15,040 --> 00:19:22,760
Introducing the CJ2A, CJ for civilian Jeep, Willys highlighted the Jeep's versatility and practicality.

231
00:19:22,761 --> 00:19:28,960
Targeting farming and industrial customers,

232
00:19:29,500 --> 00:19:33,180
the Jeep CJ could be outfitted with a host of field plows,

233
00:19:33,820 --> 00:19:35,860
compressors, or generators.

234
00:19:37,160 --> 00:19:39,760
Willys Overland's original market for the Jeep

235
00:19:39,761 --> 00:19:42,320
was primarily as an agricultural vehicle,

236
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:47,920
and their initial models were tested in agricultural roles mainly.

237
00:19:52,760 --> 00:19:57,260
The first civilian jeeps were sold as utilitarian farm vehicles.

238
00:19:57,560 --> 00:20:02,380
And, of course, what really happened is that the soldiers bought these, drive around,

239
00:20:02,520 --> 00:20:06,060
they could go, you know, through the mud and over the hills and through the forest

240
00:20:06,061 --> 00:20:10,360
and really enjoy the vehicle, and it was really the birth of the off-road sport.

241
00:20:11,020 --> 00:20:14,140
In an attempt to introduce its vehicles to a wider market,

242
00:20:14,520 --> 00:20:18,700
in 1946, Willys introduced the first all-steel station wagon.

243
00:20:19,680 --> 00:20:22,760
Like the Jeep, it was tough, reliable, and versatile.

244
00:20:23,140 --> 00:20:28,240
But with a seven-passenger seating capacity and two-wheel drive, it was better suited for families.

245
00:20:29,440 --> 00:20:33,800
In 1949, the Jeep station wagon adopted four-wheel drive,

246
00:20:34,380 --> 00:20:39,520
making it the family car that could not only do anything, it could also go anywhere.

247
00:20:40,480 --> 00:20:43,080
The modern sport utility vehicle was born.

248
00:20:43,680 --> 00:20:49,480
But it wouldn't be long before Jeep's reputation as the best 4x4 on the planet was challenged.

249
00:20:51,660 --> 00:20:56,440
The belly flopper, one of the first attempts at motorized transportation in the Army,

250
00:20:56,680 --> 00:21:02,000
could hold only two men who rode on their stomachs on a motorized platform without suspension.

251
00:21:02,120 --> 00:21:05,900
The highest speed this machine could achieve was 28 miles per hour.

252
00:21:06,400 --> 00:21:07,820
4x4 will return in a moment.

253
00:21:09,740 --> 00:21:12,720
About the time that Willys was launching the Jeep CJ,

254
00:21:13,080 --> 00:21:18,160
the Rover Car Company of Britain was devising a utilitarian 4x4 of its own.

255
00:21:19,100 --> 00:21:22,160
With Jeep the only company making consumer 4x4s,

256
00:21:22,520 --> 00:21:25,880
the market seemed right for another vehicle which could be used by farmers.

257
00:21:27,120 --> 00:21:31,140
The Wilkes brothers, who ran Rover, were very familiar with the Jeep.

258
00:21:32,040 --> 00:21:36,220
Morris Wilkes owned a farm and had actually purchased a surplus Jeep after the war.

259
00:21:36,221 --> 00:21:45,060
they had great fun playing in the sand and over the dunes with this jeep

260
00:21:45,061 --> 00:21:54,160
and spencer and his brother maurice one day looked at it and said you know this is going

261
00:21:54,161 --> 00:22:01,360
to wear out so why don't we build our own four-wheel drive vehicle the pair put together

262
00:22:01,361 --> 00:22:04,920
their first prototype using an existing Jeep chassis.

263
00:22:07,080 --> 00:22:09,900
Wilkes gave his new vehicle the name Land Rover.

264
00:22:13,160 --> 00:22:15,500
The Land Rover made a big splash,

265
00:22:16,260 --> 00:22:18,700
and the company began marketing the vehicle to farmers

266
00:22:18,701 --> 00:22:20,260
as a replacement for the horse.

267
00:22:20,920 --> 00:22:23,500
The first production models rolled off the assembly line

268
00:22:23,501 --> 00:22:28,020
in July of 1948 at a rate of 100 Land Rovers a week.

269
00:22:28,021 --> 00:22:32,260
demand for the car quickly outpaced

270
00:22:32,261 --> 00:22:34,220
Rover's modest production capacity

271
00:22:34,221 --> 00:22:40,100
in the hands of customers throughout the world

272
00:22:40,101 --> 00:22:43,380
the Land Rover proved to be the farmer's best friend

273
00:22:43,381 --> 00:22:51,060
the initial versions had power takeoffs

274
00:22:51,061 --> 00:22:54,720
and you could run saws, you could pull a plow behind them

275
00:22:54,721 --> 00:22:56,880
they used them the same as tractors

276
00:22:56,881 --> 00:23:03,420
But what they liked about it is that they could throw their sheep in, take them to the market, and bring back their supplies.

277
00:23:03,660 --> 00:23:12,320
So rather than have a tractor, they had a four-wheel drive vehicle that became very much an all-purpose conveyance in the countryside of England.

278
00:23:14,940 --> 00:23:19,760
The Land Rovers were soon embraced by fire departments, the police, and the military.

279
00:23:19,761 --> 00:23:27,320
They also became the preferred vehicle for exploration

280
00:23:27,321 --> 00:23:30,680
Traversing the world's most remote regions

281
00:23:30,681 --> 00:23:33,900
Venturing where no vehicle had ever gone before

282
00:23:33,901 --> 00:23:41,920
The vehicle was so robust and strong and simple

283
00:23:41,921 --> 00:23:43,820
Easy to repair in the field

284
00:23:43,821 --> 00:23:46,360
That it became instantly very popular

285
00:23:46,361 --> 00:23:49,240
As a matter of fact, at one point in time

286
00:23:49,241 --> 00:23:54,260
it was estimated that for 40% of the population of the Earth,

287
00:23:54,380 --> 00:23:56,780
the first vehicle they ever saw was a Land Rover.

288
00:23:57,900 --> 00:24:01,640
Its versatility also made it perfect for more leisurely pastimes,

289
00:24:01,900 --> 00:24:04,120
and a new breed of drivers soon emerged,

290
00:24:04,820 --> 00:24:06,840
eager to push their Land Rovers to the limit.

291
00:24:08,320 --> 00:24:12,020
In response, the company began sponsoring driving schools

292
00:24:12,021 --> 00:24:15,360
to teach owners how to properly drive their vehicles off-road.

293
00:24:15,361 --> 00:24:19,880
and I think if you've got an active lifestyle

294
00:24:19,881 --> 00:24:24,080
if you hunt or you shoot or you sail or you tell a horse

295
00:24:24,081 --> 00:24:26,200
then this vehicle will do it for you

296
00:24:26,201 --> 00:24:30,000
the Land Rover became popular with parents

297
00:24:30,001 --> 00:24:32,540
who could take the kids on inexpensive getaways

298
00:24:32,541 --> 00:24:37,000
the company soon rolled out the Series 2

299
00:24:37,001 --> 00:24:39,800
which further improved the Wilkes original design

300
00:24:39,801 --> 00:24:45,160
the new station wagon had more creature comforts

301
00:24:45,161 --> 00:24:48,480
and was generally more user-friendly and easier to drive for women.

302
00:24:49,600 --> 00:24:54,160
Yet underneath was the same durable, rugged design that was Land Rover's trademark.

303
00:24:56,620 --> 00:25:00,940
Land Rovers traditionally have been very forgiving vehicles to drive on the trail.

304
00:25:01,880 --> 00:25:08,100
They do what they do rather effortlessly and in an understated way,

305
00:25:08,280 --> 00:25:12,260
and you almost are amazed at what it can do after you've done it.

306
00:25:12,261 --> 00:25:19,060
Throughout the 1960s, Land Rover continued to prove itself around the world.

307
00:25:19,640 --> 00:25:22,220
Whether they were traversing uncharted territories,

308
00:25:23,120 --> 00:25:26,360
or serving in the armed forces,

309
00:25:29,460 --> 00:25:33,940
Land Rover prided itself on being able to build a vehicle for any job.

310
00:25:34,600 --> 00:25:39,040
In the 1960s, Land Rover offered over 150 different models.

311
00:25:39,700 --> 00:25:46,100
They were outfitted with cranes, winches, fire hoses, compressors, trailers, and plows.

312
00:25:47,100 --> 00:25:49,980
The British were very good at marketing the Land Rover overseas,

313
00:25:50,320 --> 00:25:53,860
and of course, you know, there was still all this British Empire stuff going on,

314
00:25:53,861 --> 00:25:58,840
and so all the countries that were maybe independent but had been British,

315
00:25:59,240 --> 00:26:02,960
they all bought Land Rovers in preference to Jeeps just because.

316
00:26:03,780 --> 00:26:07,620
But in a lot of ways, the Land Rover was superior to the Jeep of the same era.

317
00:26:08,220 --> 00:26:11,200
It had a much stouter chassis, a better suspension.

318
00:26:11,920 --> 00:26:13,120
Drive trains are about even.

319
00:26:13,480 --> 00:26:15,160
The Jeep had a little bit of edge in power.

320
00:26:15,800 --> 00:26:17,520
But the Land Rover was a good machine.

321
00:26:17,620 --> 00:26:19,600
Plus it had an aluminum body and it didn't rust.

322
00:26:21,740 --> 00:26:25,020
In America, however, the Land Rover was a rare sight.

323
00:26:26,320 --> 00:26:28,240
A few had been imported over the years,

324
00:26:28,520 --> 00:26:30,780
but they hadn't really made an impact on the market.

325
00:26:33,800 --> 00:26:37,340
The Land Rovers began to be imported to America in 1949

326
00:26:37,341 --> 00:26:39,660
in very, very small numbers.

327
00:26:40,260 --> 00:26:43,400
Obviously, they were competing against the home team here,

328
00:26:43,580 --> 00:26:45,640
you know, Jeep and other manufacturers.

329
00:26:45,641 --> 00:26:51,360
So, you know, their chances of achieving a major success here were pretty slim.

330
00:26:53,760 --> 00:26:55,660
The Jeep was still on top.

331
00:26:56,120 --> 00:26:58,100
But as the 1960s got underway,

332
00:26:58,500 --> 00:27:01,320
more Americans were discovering the joy of off-roading.

333
00:27:02,060 --> 00:27:03,800
To feed this insatiable hunger,

334
00:27:04,240 --> 00:27:06,640
a new generation of 4x4s was born

335
00:27:06,641 --> 00:27:08,680
that was geared for fun.

336
00:27:09,980 --> 00:27:13,720
The Rover Car Company of Britain, who brought us the Land Rover,

337
00:27:13,940 --> 00:27:17,720
was first known as the Coventry Sewing Machine Company in 1881.

338
00:27:18,500 --> 00:27:22,200
The first transportation vehicle built by Rover Company was a tricycle.

339
00:27:22,700 --> 00:27:27,340
From there, they produced safety bicycles, then motorcycles, and finally motorized vehicles.

340
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:29,460
4x4 will continue in a moment.

341
00:27:29,461 --> 00:27:39,900
In the 1960s, off-road enthusiasts became more concerned with recreation than utility.

342
00:27:41,580 --> 00:27:47,880
After a decade trapped in the suburbs, Americans were ready to break free and escape into the wilderness.

343
00:27:50,460 --> 00:27:53,300
More and more of these vehicles were produced and sold.

344
00:27:53,840 --> 00:27:57,860
People banded together in clubs. They put together organized trail rides.

345
00:27:59,460 --> 00:28:08,020
The Weekend Warrior was born, and groups of adventurers and families gathered together to experience the joy of outdoor living.

346
00:28:13,140 --> 00:28:20,740
One reason it became very popular was it was affordable, and it was a family outing type thing.

347
00:28:20,741 --> 00:28:25,800
You get away from your business rat race during the week, get in your four-wheel drive,

348
00:28:26,140 --> 00:28:31,680
pack on your camping equipment and your fishing pole, and go out and have some fun four-wheeling.

349
00:28:35,260 --> 00:28:43,100
To cater to this family market, International Harvester introduced a comfortable and capable 4x4, the Scout.

350
00:28:43,101 --> 00:28:52,700
To compete against the Scout, Ford introduced the Bronco in August of 1965.

351
00:28:55,340 --> 00:29:01,880
And it carried the same sport utility idea, but carried it farther than the Scout did.

352
00:29:02,280 --> 00:29:09,420
It was more comfortable, nicer to drive on the street, more powerful, with more car-like features.

353
00:29:09,421 --> 00:29:20,000
And that was the resounding theme in the sport utility movement was to get the vehicles more car-like without sacrificing their ability out on the trail.

354
00:29:21,220 --> 00:29:29,100
Land Rover responded to this challenge with their more civilized Range Rover, which was just as much at home in the city as it was in the country.

355
00:29:32,700 --> 00:29:38,560
In response, Jeep introduced the Cherokee, an evolution of their popular Wagoneer series.

356
00:29:39,420 --> 00:29:43,360
And they were all looking for the same thing.

357
00:29:43,600 --> 00:29:47,540
The American people wanted a vehicle that could haul people,

358
00:29:47,820 --> 00:29:49,960
they could fold down or take out the rear seat,

359
00:29:50,020 --> 00:29:51,840
and it could haul cargo, it might be able to tow,

360
00:29:52,160 --> 00:29:53,920
and it had to have that four-wheel drive function

361
00:29:53,921 --> 00:29:56,040
so it could take them to wherever they wanted to go.

362
00:30:00,040 --> 00:30:02,300
With the growing popularity of four-wheeling,

363
00:30:02,820 --> 00:30:05,280
drivers began to test the limits of their vehicles

364
00:30:05,281 --> 00:30:07,620
on some of the toughest trails on the planet.

365
00:30:09,420 --> 00:30:18,220
The people who love the four-wheel that are into it for the driving aspect,

366
00:30:18,221 --> 00:30:21,340
they keep wanting to increase their challenges.

367
00:30:21,560 --> 00:30:26,000
So they look for more difficult terrain, which requires vehicles that are modified.

368
00:30:26,840 --> 00:30:30,080
And then perhaps they increase their skill levels,

369
00:30:30,160 --> 00:30:32,080
and then they want to tackle more difficult terrain.

370
00:30:32,280 --> 00:30:34,820
Then they have to modify their vehicle yet again.

371
00:30:35,220 --> 00:30:37,900
And it comes to the point where if they go that far,

372
00:30:38,560 --> 00:30:42,120
They're driving vehicles that are total trailer queens.

373
00:30:42,740 --> 00:30:45,680
They're not really safe or desirable to drive on the highway,

374
00:30:46,140 --> 00:30:49,680
but they go like no tomorrow out on the trail.

375
00:30:53,680 --> 00:30:58,680
One of the most popular places for drivers to gather was the Baja Peninsula in Mexico.

376
00:30:59,660 --> 00:31:02,940
Weekend get-togethers quickly evolved into wheel-to-wheel competitions

377
00:31:02,941 --> 00:31:05,160
to see whose machine was the fastest.

378
00:31:07,900 --> 00:31:09,920
It was nothing formal.

379
00:31:10,720 --> 00:31:13,980
And, you know, we just put up some arrows around the course,

380
00:31:14,160 --> 00:31:17,840
and it was just kind of a family-type thing.

381
00:31:17,841 --> 00:31:19,800
And, you know, there was no prize money,

382
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:22,660
and I think there might have been some trophies or something like that.

383
00:31:22,800 --> 00:31:28,220
But other than that, it would just go beat your vehicle up for no basically reward.

384
00:31:30,920 --> 00:31:33,800
As interest in off-road racing continued to grow,

385
00:31:34,220 --> 00:31:37,440
drivers developed unique strategies to stay ahead of the pack.

386
00:31:37,900 --> 00:31:43,780
A friend of mine told me many years ago when I first started racing off-road,

387
00:31:43,840 --> 00:31:45,820
you have to learn when to go fast.

388
00:31:46,020 --> 00:31:48,400
There are times to go fast, there are times not to go fast.

389
00:31:48,800 --> 00:31:49,820
Don't get the two mixed up.

390
00:31:50,560 --> 00:31:54,440
In a road race, for example, you continually have to go fast to stay up with the crowd.

391
00:31:55,220 --> 00:31:57,020
In off-road racing, you're racing the terrain.

392
00:31:58,400 --> 00:32:03,440
One of the most challenging events was the Baja 1000, which began in 1967.

393
00:32:03,441 --> 00:32:09,940
This rugged endurance race attracted such legendary drivers as Parnelli Jones and Ivan Stewart.

394
00:32:10,940 --> 00:32:19,140
This bone-jarring event snakes its way through dusty villages, over boulder-strewn trails, and along the rugged coastline of Baja, Mexico.

395
00:32:19,880 --> 00:32:23,200
It's exciting for the drivers, as well as their team of mechanics.

396
00:32:26,840 --> 00:32:32,760
If you've never done it, there's a lure of adventure of just coming out here and being out in the middle of nowhere.

397
00:32:32,761 --> 00:32:37,660
and just helping somebody make their way down the peninsula.

398
00:32:37,960 --> 00:32:38,860
You know, it's...

399
00:32:38,861 --> 00:32:41,600
Don't know any other way to describe it.

400
00:32:41,780 --> 00:32:43,280
It's just exciting.

401
00:32:44,640 --> 00:32:48,520
I've heard drivers in that race call it a 12-hour adrenaline rush.

402
00:32:49,400 --> 00:32:50,540
It is incredible.

403
00:32:50,700 --> 00:32:52,300
There's times where you can't see clearly,

404
00:32:52,460 --> 00:32:54,540
and you're going 75, 100 miles an hour,

405
00:32:55,060 --> 00:32:58,400
and you're not sure that you're on the right path either.

406
00:32:58,401 --> 00:33:01,540
There's so many converging trails and tracks out there.

407
00:33:02,120 --> 00:33:06,660
But it's very exciting, and it is a constant test of driver and machine.

408
00:33:10,260 --> 00:33:17,440
In November of 2000, more than 263 entrants gathered in Ensenada for the start of the Baja 2000.

409
00:33:21,540 --> 00:33:26,640
Nearly twice as long as previous events, the event drew spectators from around the world

410
00:33:26,641 --> 00:33:30,320
who line the dusty course from Ensenada to Cabo San Lucas

411
00:33:30,321 --> 00:33:32,440
and cheer on their favorite drivers.

412
00:33:37,900 --> 00:33:42,600
Any other form of racing, if it's a drag race or if it's Indianapolis,

413
00:33:42,960 --> 00:33:46,960
if it's a road circuit in Atlanta, that surface is going to be pretty much constant.

414
00:33:47,320 --> 00:33:50,540
We're going to run a 2,000-mile event from Ensenada to Cabo San Lucas,

415
00:33:50,700 --> 00:33:56,400
and not one turn, not one bump, not one jump will be identical to the last one.

416
00:33:56,640 --> 00:34:00,000
I mean, it's the ultimate experience in motorsports, in my opinion.

417
00:34:01,740 --> 00:34:04,260
While the drivers get the lion's share of the glory,

418
00:34:05,020 --> 00:34:08,700
they wouldn't get far without the help of the unsung heroes of the pit crews,

419
00:34:09,380 --> 00:34:11,880
who risk their own safety to keep the cars going.

420
00:34:14,240 --> 00:34:16,660
We always have the possibility of somebody getting hurt.

421
00:34:16,740 --> 00:34:19,020
You've got a fire probability when you're pouring fuel.

422
00:34:19,500 --> 00:34:21,720
You've got your jacking heavy cars up.

423
00:34:21,860 --> 00:34:23,060
You could fall off a jack.

424
00:34:23,120 --> 00:34:24,860
You could have somebody underneath the car at one time.

425
00:34:24,861 --> 00:34:27,860
We stress safety immensely.

426
00:34:28,580 --> 00:34:30,140
We've got a guy at stop sign in the front.

427
00:34:30,240 --> 00:34:31,380
We've got a guy in the rear watching.

428
00:34:31,760 --> 00:34:34,680
Before any car is released, we make sure everybody's clear.

429
00:34:39,980 --> 00:34:44,880
It's still very much an adventure, this race especially.

430
00:34:45,600 --> 00:34:50,680
You'd think they are taking these vehicles down to Cabo San Lucas, almost 2,000 miles.

431
00:34:51,340 --> 00:34:53,820
It's been raining the last few days.

432
00:34:53,821 --> 00:35:04,717
And everything that gets thrown in your way, whether it be mechanical or you get tired, your drivers get tired, your chase crews get tired and don't show up, it's

433
00:35:04,718 --> 00:35:08,080
truly one of the very last motorsports adventures.

434
00:35:13,460 --> 00:35:23,060
While surviving the Baja is a tremendous feat, the Paris-Dakar rally is considered by many to be the most prestigious off-road race in the world.

435
00:35:23,820 --> 00:35:35,260
This rally spans over 6,000 miles in 20 days, covering some of the most inhospitable landscapes on the planet.

436
00:35:42,160 --> 00:35:51,260
Running flat out through the desert, without road signs or trail markers, drivers are lucky to even finish this grueling race.

437
00:35:53,820 --> 00:36:04,280
Those who have completed the Paris-Dakar rally, which ends at the foot of the Egyptian pyramids,

438
00:36:04,780 --> 00:36:07,920
know they've met the ultimate challenge and survived.

439
00:36:13,240 --> 00:36:17,260
Speed, however, isn't always the absolute goal of four-wheelers.

440
00:36:17,820 --> 00:36:22,280
Some off-roaders are more interested in testing just how high their vehicles can take them.

441
00:36:23,820 --> 00:36:34,880
Well, rock crawling is, you know, rather than actually built trails,

442
00:36:35,580 --> 00:36:42,320
the guys who like rock crawling will tend to try and go up or down just about anything that's out there.

443
00:36:42,900 --> 00:36:45,640
The vehicles they have are substantially modified.

444
00:36:45,641 --> 00:36:56,880
They have large suspension articulation so they can basically crawl their vehicles over the rocks without smashing up the bottoms or the sides.

445
00:36:57,620 --> 00:37:00,780
It's a little more than I get into.

446
00:37:03,360 --> 00:37:08,360
Well, rock crawling is kind of like driving places where vehicles weren't meant to go.

447
00:37:09,200 --> 00:37:13,040
Rock crawling basically is driving over six-foot-high boulders,

448
00:37:13,620 --> 00:37:19,300
a series of six-foot-high boulders through narrow cracks that vehicles barely fit,

449
00:37:19,900 --> 00:37:21,720
and basically doing the impossible.

450
00:37:25,440 --> 00:37:28,740
While these rock hoppers don't always make it to the top,

451
00:37:29,620 --> 00:37:33,840
there is another breed of 4x4 that can't afford to fail.

452
00:37:33,841 --> 00:37:41,580
The Jeep was showing its age, and the military launched a search for a rugged, go-anywhere replacement.

453
00:37:42,380 --> 00:37:44,520
The Humvee was about to be born.

454
00:37:46,300 --> 00:37:52,080
The original Whippet car got its name from the Whippet dog because the cars were small yet fast.

455
00:37:52,800 --> 00:37:57,340
The Willys Overland Company, who eventually paired with Ford to mass-produce Jeeps for the Army,

456
00:37:57,820 --> 00:38:00,060
used the Whippet as the basis of the Jeep design.

457
00:38:00,720 --> 00:38:02,300
4x4 will return in a moment.

458
00:38:03,840 --> 00:38:09,600
With the 1980s and 90s came an explosion in the 4x4 sport utility market.

459
00:38:11,400 --> 00:38:15,100
In addition to such stalwarts as the Jeep and the Land Rover,

460
00:38:15,560 --> 00:38:21,400
new vehicles arrived from Honda, Lexus, Mercedes, Dodge, and even Cadillac.

461
00:38:25,080 --> 00:38:27,780
Sport utility vehicles began to really take off,

462
00:38:28,220 --> 00:38:31,580
not so much because people wanted the recreational lifestyle,

463
00:38:31,581 --> 00:38:36,060
but because they wanted to differentiate themselves from the cars,

464
00:38:36,460 --> 00:38:39,020
which, due to Congress computers in the wind tunnel,

465
00:38:39,120 --> 00:38:42,040
had all become sort of androgynous clones.

466
00:38:42,220 --> 00:38:43,420
They all looked the same.

467
00:38:45,560 --> 00:38:49,700
Whereas Jeep and Land Rover had built a reputation with off-road endurance,

468
00:38:50,320 --> 00:38:53,220
these new vehicles were targeted at suburban drivers

469
00:38:53,221 --> 00:38:56,420
and came in a wide variety of sizes and configurations.

470
00:39:01,580 --> 00:39:07,420
With so many SUVs on city streets and suburban neighborhoods,

471
00:39:08,120 --> 00:39:11,960
it was only a matter of time before owners of these go-anywhere vehicles

472
00:39:11,961 --> 00:39:13,960
decided to put them to the test.

473
00:39:17,500 --> 00:39:21,220
New Jeep owners found that they could participate in jamborees,

474
00:39:21,460 --> 00:39:24,380
where they could discover the joy of off-roading.

475
00:39:24,381 --> 00:39:36,680
For Land Rover owners, club rallies, a long-time tradition with international owners, are now being held for American drivers.

476
00:39:39,680 --> 00:39:49,380
These events, along with others, let drivers experience the full capabilities of four-wheel drive and showed them what these vehicles were originally designed to do.

477
00:39:50,380 --> 00:39:55,660
There's literally a group in every city of size, a club, a four-wheel drive club, off-road club.

478
00:39:56,180 --> 00:40:00,480
There's a lot of opportunities for someone that hasn't gone off-roading before to get involved,

479
00:40:00,780 --> 00:40:07,020
to use the vehicle that they probably already own to shuttle kids around, all the soccer team to games.

480
00:40:07,660 --> 00:40:10,660
And here you can set aside a weekend every month, every other month,

481
00:40:10,661 --> 00:40:14,300
and go out and learn the real capabilities of the vehicle and use it off-road.

482
00:40:15,380 --> 00:40:18,020
Consumer 4x4s had come a long way.

483
00:40:18,021 --> 00:40:22,740
But for the ultimate in off-roading, the military still had the competition beat.

484
00:40:23,460 --> 00:40:26,520
In 1979, with the Jeep showing its age,

485
00:40:26,840 --> 00:40:33,140
the Army once again set out to build a high-mobility, multipurpose wheeled vehicle, or Humvee.

486
00:40:33,580 --> 00:40:35,000
It needed to be agile.

487
00:40:35,500 --> 00:40:38,160
It needed to be able to go places that defied imagination.

488
00:40:38,920 --> 00:40:43,940
And it needed to last a minimum of 12 years of the toughest treatment imaginable.

489
00:40:43,941 --> 00:40:51,200
The task to deliver this new vehicle was given to the AM General Corporation.

490
00:40:51,980 --> 00:40:56,940
And what they came up with was arguably the most versatile military vehicle in the world.

491
00:41:01,700 --> 00:41:05,620
The first images of these state-of-the-art vehicles came out of Panama,

492
00:41:06,200 --> 00:41:10,220
where they were used for everything from launching missiles to transporting the moon.

493
00:41:13,940 --> 00:41:19,740
Yet the Humvee earned its real reputation in the Gulf War,

494
00:41:20,200 --> 00:41:23,080
where it endured some of the most punishing treatment imaginable.

495
00:41:23,600 --> 00:41:28,260
And, like the Jeep before it, the Humvee became the soldier's best friend,

496
00:41:28,600 --> 00:41:32,440
as well as the envy of off-road enthusiasts around the world.

497
00:41:32,940 --> 00:41:35,760
Ever since we first introduced the vehicle to the military,

498
00:41:36,500 --> 00:41:39,700
we got queries from individuals, civilians.

499
00:41:40,440 --> 00:41:41,980
How can I get one of these?

500
00:41:41,981 --> 00:41:46,320
They have tremendous appeal to people on an almost magical level.

501
00:41:48,880 --> 00:41:54,700
Even with a starting price of over $75,000, dealers couldn't keep Hummers on the lot.

502
00:41:55,400 --> 00:42:00,620
There aren't a lot of people that can logically put something like that to use.

503
00:42:01,140 --> 00:42:07,460
If you're an outfitter or, you know, run a ranch or something like that, I can see a use for it.

504
00:42:07,461 --> 00:42:14,300
But for somebody who wants to have a vehicle that they can drive to work and then take out on the trail, I don't think that's the one for you.

505
00:42:15,460 --> 00:42:21,840
Whether the Hummer is your cup of tea or not, no one can deny the impact the military has had on four-wheeling.

506
00:42:24,380 --> 00:42:30,940
The military really drove four-wheel drive development through the early parts of the 20th century.

507
00:42:31,260 --> 00:42:35,220
But now four-wheel drive is a real big part of the civilian world.

508
00:42:36,100 --> 00:42:41,800
So the military developments and the civilian developments are really on two different tracks.

509
00:42:42,300 --> 00:42:48,200
Today we have a variety of choices in SUVs, from traditional to upscale luxury models.

510
00:42:48,820 --> 00:42:53,180
The one thing they all share is that most of them will never be taken off-roading.

511
00:42:55,020 --> 00:42:58,220
60% of trucks sold in the United States right now are four-wheel drive,

512
00:42:58,640 --> 00:43:02,680
and yet how many people really use them, have ever engaged four-wheel drive on these?

513
00:43:03,140 --> 00:43:06,100
I think the reason for that is that we like capability.

514
00:43:06,360 --> 00:43:10,040
We like the comfort of knowing that we can do it.

515
00:43:11,180 --> 00:43:14,420
Off-roading has come a long way in the last 100 years,

516
00:43:14,560 --> 00:43:16,680
but one thing has remained the same.

517
00:43:17,200 --> 00:43:20,020
These go-anywhere vehicles continue to excite

518
00:43:20,021 --> 00:43:24,400
and inspire us to see what lies over the next horizon.

519
00:43:24,401 --> 00:43:34,020
